          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression is a direct first-person, present-tense avowal of love. Goneril’s continuing comparisons intensify the asserted degree of that emotional state.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The occurrence asserts the magnitude of Goneril’s filial love but does not itself promise conduct, renew an undertaking, or position her as bound by a specified or invoked obligation. The surrounding turn remains an extended comparative avowal. Lear’s subsequent grant is uptake of her answer in the love competition, not acceptance of a commitment made by these words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Of all these bounds, even from this line to this,\\nWith shadowy forests and with champains rich’d,\\nWith plenteous rivers and wide-skirted meads,\\nWe make thee lady:\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression is delivered as the opening of an elaborate, formally constructed answer to Lear’s solicitation, rather than as a reflex-like affective discharge. Its intensity is part of sustained rhetoric and does not independently establish expressive eruption.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\\n\\nGONERIL.\\nSir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing affection, the expression functions substantially as a competitive rhetorical claim: Lear has explicitly tied the daughters’ professed degree of love to the size of his bounty, and Goneril answers by claiming superlative love. It therefore operates as praise and self-positioning for the largest reward, though it contains no explicit request.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most and explicitly connects their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Goneril, called upon first, addresses Lear with the focal avowal and an extended declaration of unsurpassable filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No distinct undertaking by Goneril is established through the focal occurrence or its own turn. Lear treats the declaration as a satisfactory answer and immediately awards Goneril and Albany territory, but that is reward-based uptake of her professed degree of love rather than acceptance of a promise. The supplied passage does not settle Goneril’s sincerity or actual affection.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage is recognised as the opening love-test scene of William Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence supports analysis of the utterance’s public, competitive force but does not establish Goneril’s private sincerity, actual emotional state, or any unstated intended obligations. Cordelia’s skeptical and contrasting remarks supply interpretive context but cannot substitute for Goneril’s own speech act.\"}"
        }
